    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 271725\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1025 AM PDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Dry and seasonable weather conditions through at least Thursday.\n\n* Precipitation chances and cooler temperatures will return to the\n  region later this week, continuing through the weekend. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through Monday.\n\nZonal flow maintains mostly quiet weather conditions and mild \ntemperatures for the next couple of days. A weak shortwave moving \nalong this pattern will prompt breezy southerly winds across much of \nthe area this afternoon, with peak gusts between 15 and 25 mph. \nTemperatures will be within a few degrees of normal through \nThursday, which includes highs in the upper 70s for Las Vegas, the \nlow 80s in the Colorado River Valley, and the upper 60s to low 70s \nin Owens Valley. \n\nA pattern shift arrives later this week as a longwave trough \napproaches the West Coast. The initial push of moisture will occur on \nThursday and Friday as Tropical Storm Vance travels up the Gulf of \nCalifornia ahead of the trough and PWATS rise to upwards of 200 \npercent of normal. Precipitation chances on Thursday night into \nFriday favor northwestern Arizona and southeastern Nevada with POPs \nin the 20 to 60 percent range, particularly over high terrain. \nHowever, more substantial moisture advection will take place on \nSaturday and Sunday as the trough moves inland and draws subtropical \nmoisture into the region. There is still a good amount of \ndiscrepancy between models regarding the trajectory of this \nmoisture, which will heavily influence precipitation amounts. If the \nsouthwesterly flow solution plays out, the rain shadow will limit \nprecipitation totals across the Mojave Desert. However, moisture \nwill reach far further inland if the flow takes a more southerly \ndirection. The presence of an embedded shortwave also has the \npotential to boost precipitation totals. Given these uncertainties, \nplease continue to monitor the forecast for updates over the next \nfew days. \n\n&&\n \n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package...\nLight easterly winds this morning become SE early this afternoon. \nSpeeds should remain less than 8 knots before 22-23z then increase \nonce the S-SSW shift occurs with gusts of 20 to 25 kt. Gusts persist \nthrough sunset before speeds diminish to 8 knots or less for the \novernight period. VFR conditions will prevail through the TAF \nperiod, with mainly clear skies into the afternoon before SCT high \nclouds with bases AOA 20kft AGL spread into the area during the \nevening and overnight. A similar diurnal wind pattern is expected \nagain tomorrow. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Winds will follow \ntypical diurnal directional trends today with light winds through \nthe morning before speeds increase with gusts to 20 kt in most areas \nduring the mid afternoon and evening. Lighter winds return for the \novernight period. VFR conditions will prevail through the TAF \nperiod, with SCT high clouds overspreading the area from west to \neast later this afternoon through tonight. A similar diurnal wind \npattern is expected again tomorrow.  \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Meltzer \nAVIATION...Moore\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage: \nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
